To determine if the product $ f(x)g(x) $ is even, odd, or neither, we can use the definitions of even and odd functions. 

1. An even function satisfies $ f(-x) = f(x) $ for all $ x $. Similarly, $ g(-x) = g(x) $.
2. An odd function satisfies $ f(-x) = -f(x) $ for all $ x $. Similarly, $ g(-x) = -g(x) $.

Since both $ f(x) $ and $ g(x) $ are even functions, we have:
$$
f(-x) = f(x) \quad \text{and} \quad g(-x) = g(x)
$$

Now, consider the product $ f(x)g(x) $. Substitute $ -x $ into the product:
$$
f(-x)g(-x) = f(x)g(x)
$$

Substituting the even function properties:
$$
f(-x)g(-x) = f(x)g(x)
$$

Since the product of two even functions is also even, the product $ f(x)g(x) $ is even. Therefore, the answer is even.
